          "case_name": "Greene County Juvenile Office v. K.J.C.",
          "citation": "382 S.W.3d 193 (Mo. Ct. App. 2012)",
          "court": "Missouri Court of Appeals",
          "year": 2012,
          "date_decided": "2012-09-11",
          "holding": "The court reversed, holding that the evidence did not clearly and convincingly show the children were without proper care, custody or support, so the juvenile court erred in taking jurisdiction and removing them from an arguably over-protective but not abusive mother.",
          "relevance": "The reversal effectively ends the case, which is the legal content of a dismissal for insufficient evidence.",
          "quote": "the trial court must find by clear and convincing evidence that the child is without proper care, custody, or support",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5283254/greene-county-juvenile-office-v-kjc/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 5283254
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In the Interest of J.T.S.",
          "citation": "462 S.W.3d 475 (Mo. Ct. App. 2015)",
          "court": "Missouri Court of Appeals",
          "year": 2015,
          "date_decided": "2015-06-02",
          "holding": "The court dismissed the mother's appeal from an adjudication and disposition judgment as moot, because the juvenile court's jurisdiction over the child had ended before the appeal was decided.",
          "relevance": "The decision illustrates that when jurisdiction ends and the case closes, the appellate courts will not reach the merits of the underlying finding.",
          "quote": "We dismiss the appeal because it is moot.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2823060/in-the-interest-of-jts-juvenile-officer-v-hjs-mother/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2823060
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Sercl v. Director, Department of Social Services, Children's Division",
          "citation": "477 S.W.3d 85 (Mo. Ct. App. 2015)",
          "court": "Missouri Court of Appeals",
          "year": 2015,
          "date_decided": "2015-07-22",
          "holding": "The court affirmed judgment for the alleged perpetrator, holding that the Division bears the burden to prove neglect by a preponderance of the evidence before a name may be placed on the Central Registry and that a judgment against the party with the burden need not be supported by substantial evidence.",
          "relevance": "The decision is the clearest Missouri statement of why a case ends when the agency fails to meet its burden, rather than the accused having to disprove the allegation.",
          "quote": "assign to the Division the burden to prove by a preponderance of the evidence that Sercl neglected K.J. by failing to provide her with the care necessary for her well-being",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2822930/in-the-interest-of-daryl-sercl-petitioner-respondent-v-director/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2822930
        }
